      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2015 Society for the Study of Addiction.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "AIMS",
            "content": "To determine if associations of alcohol consumption with all-cause mortality replicate in discordant monozygotic twin comparisons that control for familial and genetic confounds."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "DESIGN",
            "content": "A 30-year prospective follow-up."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "SETTING",
            "content": "Population-based older Finnish twin cohort."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "PARTICIPANTS",
            "content": "Same-sex twins, aged 24-60\u2009years at the end of 1981, without overt comorbidities, completed questionnaires in 1975 and 1981 with response rates of 89 and 84%. A total of 15,607 twins were available for mortality follow-up from the date of returned 1981 questionnaires to 31\u2009December 2011; 14,787 twins with complete information were analysed."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "MEASUREMENTS",
            "content": "Self-reported monthly alcohol consumption, heavy drinking occasions (HDO) and alcohol-induced blackouts. Adjustments for age, gender, marital and smoking status, physical activity, obesity, education and social class."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "FINDINGS",
            "content": "Among twins as individuals, high levels of monthly alcohol consumption (≥\u2009259\u2009g/month) associated with earlier mortality [hazard ratio (HR)\u2009=\u20091.63, 95% confidence interval (CI)\u2009=\u20091.47-1.81]. That association was replicated in comparisons of all informatively drinking-discordant twin pairs (HR\u2009=\u20091.91, 95% CI\u2009=\u20091.49-2.45) and within discordant monozygotic (MZ) twin pairs (HR\u2009=\u20092.24, 95% CI\u2009=\u20091.31-3.85), with comparable effect size. Smaller samples of MZ twins discordant for HDO and blackouts limited power; a significant association with mortality was found for multiple blackouts (HR\u2009=\u20092.82, 95% CI\u2009=\u20091.30-6.08), but not for HDO."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "The associations of high levels of monthly alcohol consumption and alcohol-induced blackouts with increased all-cause mortality among Finnish twins cannot be explained by familial or genetic confounds; the explanation appears to be causal."
          }
        ]
      },
